====================================================================
ORACLE vs OBSERVED
====================================================================

ADR 0005 predictions for each graph (classes / non-trivial merges):

  5-chain               3 classes, middle-middle class has 2 edges      ✓
  bidirectional (n=4)   3 classes, each with 2 edges                    ✓
  3-cycle               1 class with 3 edges                            ✓
  out-star (3 spokes)   1 class with 3 edges                            ✓
  in-star (3 spokes)    1 class with 3 edges                            ✓
  disjoint 2×3-chain    2 classes, each with 2 edges (cross-component)  ✓

All six predictions match. ADR 0005 is validated on this suite.

====================================================================
OBSERVATIONS
====================================================================

1. The first appearance of "repetition inside a single graph."
   Identifier-level equivalence (ADR 0004) showed repetition only for
   graphs with inherent symmetry (cycles, stars). The 5-chain's three
   middle nodes also collapsed, but the edges between them did not —
   until now. Edge-level signatures produce the {R(a2,a3), R(a3,a4)}
   merge, which is the first case where a *single* graph contributes
   a multi-member class from the "interior" rather than from pure
   symmetry. This is the signal a later pattern-mining layer can use:
   "when |class| > 1, there is repetition; repetition is the footprint
   of a recurring local structure."

2. Direction is preserved, as required.
   The bidirectional chain produces three distinct classes:
     out-from-end  = (Both-1-1, Both-2-2)
     in-to-end     = (Both-2-2, Both-1-1)
     middle-middle = (Both-2-2, Both-2-2)
   The first two have the same endpoint-profile *multiset* but differ in
   order, which the ordered pair preserves. Commitment 2 is intact.

3. Stars become "one edge type repeated k times."
   Both the out-star and in-star collapse to a single class containing
   every spoke. The whole "star-ness" is reducible (at this layer) to
   "many copies of one edge type." A compound-pattern mechanism could
   pick this up as: "star of size k = k instances of edge class C, all
   sharing endpoint x with signature σ_hub and disjoint endpoints with
   signature σ_leaf." This hints at how compound pattern *definitions*
   might be built.

4. Disjoint unions merge for edges too.
   The two disjoint 3-chains produce exactly the same class structure
   as one 3-chain would, but with each class populated twice. This
   keeps the "same structural role across locations" property consistent
   with the identifier layer, which is good for downstream mining.

5. The 3-cycle is indistinguishable from "three copies of a single
   edge type" on this layer.
   The cycle and an out-star share no structural similarity, yet each
   collapses to a one-class situation at this layer. The difference is
   visible only when we consider *which* nodes the edges connect — i.e.,
   when we look at *co-occurrence* of edge instances at shared
   identifiers. This is a concrete motivation for the next step.

====================================================================
WHAT THIS ENABLES / WHAT IT DOES NOT
====================================================================

Enables:
  - Edge-level role clustering — foundational for mining.
  - "Repetition inside a single graph" becomes visible as class size > 1.
  - Stars and chains have different class-size signatures (star: one
    big class; chain: one small merge class between two singletons),
    providing a coarse graph-level fingerprint even before pattern
    naming.

Does NOT enable (yet):
  - Naming the compound pattern as a first-class unit. Per commitment 3
    this must happen as a meta-R instance, but we have no mechanism
    yet to *create* that meta-R.
  - Distinguishing "one long chain" from "many short ones sharing no
    identifiers" — both produce the same edge-class structure (modulo
    class sizes). Needs co-occurrence / locality.
  - Distinguishing cycles from stars at this layer — both collapse to
    one class. Needs the locality signal above.

====================================================================
DECISION / NEXT
====================================================================

ADR 0005 edge-level signature is sufficient for the immediate purpose:
it produces the "repetition in a single graph" signal that pattern
mining needs.

Next layer candidates, roughly in increasing ambition:

  (α) Locality / co-occurrence structure:
      "Which edge classes share an identifier?" An adjacency over edge
      classes, naturally derivable from the existing machinery. Would
      distinguish cycle from star (cycle: same class chains end-to-end
      via shared identifiers; star: all edges share exactly the hub).

  (β) Compound pattern as a named meta-R instance (commitment 3):
      A pattern P is a new identifier in the R space. The pattern's
      "instances" are encoded by new R instances, e.g., R(P, R_i) for
      each member R_i. Open: what shape connects the pattern to its
      structural definition (the member-edge-class vocabulary).

  (γ) Self-drive: what triggers trying to name a pattern?
      Candidates: class size exceeding a threshold; a locality cluster
      that repeats; a compression (MDL) gain from replacing raw edges
      with a single pattern token.

Recommendation: go with (α) first. It is cheap, completes the
"locality" axis that this ADR identified as missing, and provides the
raw structural signal (β) will need to attach names to.
